\section{Introduction \& System Overview}

In underwater acoustics and naval hydrodynamics, determining the precise spatial coordinates $(x, y, z)$ of hydrophone array elements is essential for beamforming, target localization, and bearing estimation. Traditional kinematic estimators—such as the discrete tractrix model implemented in routines like \texttt{chainlink.m}—rely solely on towing vessel (ownship) motion. They treat the array as a friction-free follow-the-leader chain dragged behind the vessel.

While kinematic pursuit models predict array behavior reasonably well on steady courses, they suffer from significant lag and trajectory inaccuracies during ship maneuvers. Moreover, simple 2D models completely ignore vertical deflection, hydrodynamic droop, and pitch variations.

To overcome these limitations, modern towed systems embed \textbf{Non-Acoustic Sensor (NAS)} nodes along the array cable. Each node measures local physical orientations (heading, pitch, roll) and hydrostatic pressure. This chapter provides a rigorous mathematical formulation for reconstructing the full 3D shape of an array directly from discrete NAS telemetry.

