\section{Coordinate Systems \& Tangent Vector Formulations}

We define a global right-handed East-North-Down (END) coordinate system centered at a suitable geodetic origin:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item $x$-axis: Direction of East ($\text{m}$)
    \item $y$-axis: Direction of North ($\text{m}$)
    \item $z$-axis: Depth below surface ($\text{m}$, positive downwards)
\end{itemize}

Let $s \in [0, L]$ denote the spatial arc length along the array cable, measured from the cable/array junction (the array nose, $s = 0$) aft toward the tail ($s = L$).

At any point $s$ along the array, the local forward unit vector $\mathbf{f}(s)$ points forward (toward the ship). In terms of true heading $\psi(s)$ (clockwise from North in radians) and pitch angle $\theta(s)$ (positive nose-up in radians), $\mathbf{f}(s)$ is defined as:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{f}(s) = \begin{bmatrix} f_x(s) \\[0.2em] f_y(s) \\[0.2em] f_z(s) \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} \cos\theta(s) \sin\psi(s) \\[0.2em] \cos\theta(s) \cos\psi(s) \\[0.2em] -\sin\theta(s) \end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}

Because arc length $s$ is measured \textbf{aft} (from nose to tail), the spatial derivative of the position vector $\mathbf{r}(s) = [x(s), y(s), z(s)]^T$ points opposite to the forward unit vector $\mathbf{f}(s)$:
\begin{equation}
\frac{d\mathbf{r}}{ds} = -\mathbf{f}(s) = \begin{bmatrix} -\cos\theta(s) \sin\psi(s) \\[0.2em] -\cos\theta(s) \cos\psi(s) \\[0.2em] \sin\theta(s) \end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}

\section{Continuous Spatial Integration Theory}

By Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, integrating the differential vector $d\mathbf{r}/ds$ from the nose position $\mathbf{r}_0 = [x_0, y_0, z_0]^T$ at $s = 0$ out to any arbitrary arc length $s$ yields the continuous spatial position equations:

\begin{align}
x(s) &= x_0 - \int_{0}^{s} \cos\theta(\sigma) \sin\psi(\sigma) \, d\sigma \label{eq:x_int} \\[0.5em]
y(s) &= y_0 - \int_{0}^{s} \cos\theta(\sigma) \cos\psi(\sigma) \, d\sigma \label{eq:y_int} \\[0.5em]
z_{\text{int}}(s) &= z_0 + \int_{0}^{s} \sin\theta(\sigma) \, d\sigma \label{eq:z_int}
\end{align}

Where $z_{\text{int}}(s)$ represents the uncorrected integrated depth profile obtained strictly from the pitch sensor integration.

\section{Discrete Numerical Integration Algorithm}

In practice, orientation sensors are deployed at $M$ discrete nodes along the array at arc lengths $s_1 < s_2 < \dots < s_M$. To compute array element locations on a fine discretization grid $s_0, s_1, \dots, s_N$ with sub-interval step sizes $\Delta s_k = s_k - s_{k-1}$, we employ a second-order Midpoint Integration Scheme.

\subsection{Phase Angle Unwrapping \& Interpolation}
Because raw compass headings wrap across the $[0, 2\pi)$ boundary, heading values must first be unwrapped to produce a continuous function $\psi(s)$. Angle profiles $\psi(s)$ and $\theta(s)$ are interpolated across node locations using Piecewise Cubic Hermite Interpolating Polynomials (\texttt{pchip}) to maintain smooth derivatives without unphysical overshoots.

\subsection{Midpoint Vector Integration}
For a segment bounded by $s_{k-1}$ and $s_k$, the effective midpoint angles are:
\begin{equation}
\bar{\psi}_k = \frac{\psi(s_{k-1}) + \psi(s_k)}{2}, \qquad \bar{\theta}_k = \frac{\theta(s_{k-1}) + \theta(s_k)}{2}
\end{equation}

The incremental spatial steps $(\Delta x_k, \Delta y_k, \Delta z_k)$ over distance $\Delta s_k$ are given by:
\begin{align}
\Delta x_k &= -\Delta s_k \cos(\bar{\theta}_k) \sin(\bar{\psi}_k) \\
\Delta y_k &= -\Delta s_k \cos(\bar{\theta}_k) \cos(\bar{\psi}_k) \\
\Delta z_k &= +\Delta s_k \sin(\bar{\theta}_k)
\end{align}

Accumulating these increments gives discrete grid coordinates:
\begin{equation}
x_k = x_0 + \sum_{j=1}^{k} \Delta x_j, \qquad y_k = y_0 + \sum_{j=1}^{k} \Delta y_j, \qquad z_{\text{int}, k} = z_0 + \sum_{j=1}^{k} \Delta z_j
\end{equation}

\section{Hydrostatic Depth Pinning \& Residual Correction}

Direct numerical integration of pitch sensor readings accumulates biases and low-frequency noise over long arrays ($L \approx 500\text{ m}$), leading to vertical drift. To eliminate this accumulation error, hydrostatic pressure readings $P_j$ measured at each NAS node are converted to true physical depth $z_{\text{meas}, j}$.

\subsection{Pressure-to-Depth Conversion}
Hydrostatic pressure $P$ at depth $z$ is expressed as:
\begin{equation}
P = P_{\text{atm}} + \rho \, g \, z
\end{equation}
Solving for physical depth $z_{\text{meas}, j}$ at node $j$:
\begin{equation}
z_{\text{meas}, j} = \frac{P_j - P_{\text{atm}}}{\rho \, g}
\end{equation}
where $\rho \approx 1025\text{ kg/m}^3$ is seawater density, $g = 9.81\text{ m/s}^2$ is gravitational acceleration, and $P_{\text{atm}} = 101,325\text{ Pa}$ is standard atmospheric pressure.

\subsection{Residual Interpolation \& Depth Fusion}
At each NAS node location $s_j$, the depth residual $r_j$ between the pressure measurement and the integrated pitch depth is evaluated:
\begin{equation}
r_j = z_{\text{meas}, j} - z_{\text{int}}(s_j)
\end{equation}

This residual vector $\mathbf{r} = [r_1, r_2, \dots, r_M]^T$ is interpolated linearly across all array arc lengths $s$ to form a smooth correction profile $c(s)$. The final pinned depth $z_{\text{final}}(s)$ across all acoustic elements is:
\begin{equation}
z_{\text{final}}(s) = z_{\text{int}}(s) + c(s)
\end{equation}

Notice that $c(s_1)$ automatically absorbs any initial uncertainty in the nose depth $z_0$, effectively tying the entire vertical array shape directly to absolute pressure references.

